# Structured concurrency

## The unstructured mess

```java
Future<User>   u = pool.submit(() -> fetchUser(id));
Future<Orders> o = pool.submit(() -> fetchOrders(id));
User user = u.get();   // if this throws, o keeps running — a leak
```

## Subtasks scoped like a block

```java
try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope.ShutdownOnFailure()) {
    Subtask<User>   u = scope.fork(() -> fetchUser(id));
    Subtask<Orders> o = scope.fork(() -> fetchOrders(id));
    scope.join();               // wait for BOTH
    scope.throwIfFailed();      // propagate first failure
    return new Page(u.get(), o.get());
}   // no subtask is still running
```

## What the scope guarantees

- **All-or-nothing** — scope won't exit with a subtask still alive; no orphans
- **Auto-cancel** — one fails → siblings cancelled (`ShutdownOnSuccess` = first win)
- **Error propagation** — one `try/catch` around the scope

**Structure of the code = structure of the work.** (Preview, built on virtual threads.)
